-- title:
--   Injectivity of specialisation on n-torsion over a smooth base
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a discrete valuation ring which is a domain, $K$ a field that is its fraction field, $B$ a scheme and $g \colon B \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ a morphism. Let `LB` be a relative group law for $g$ over $R$: for every scheme $T$ and every $t \colon T \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ a multiplication, unit and inverse on the set $\{\varphi \colon T \to B \mid \varphi \text{ followed by } g = t\}$ of points of $B$ over $t$, satisfying associativity, both unit laws and left inverse, and natural in $T$ under precomposition with morphisms over $\operatorname{Spec} R$. Assume `hcomm`, that all these multiplications are commutative, and `hN`, that $g$ is smooth, separated, locally of finite type and quasi-compact and has the Néron extension property relative to $K$: for every scheme $T$ and every smooth $t \colon T \to \operatorname{Spec} R$, the generic-fibre restriction map `genericFibreRestrict R K g t` is bijective. Let $n$ be a natural number whose image in $R$ is a unit. Let $R'$ be a domain and $R$-algebra such that $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $R \to R'$ is smooth, let $L$ be a field which is an $R'$-algebra, a $K$-algebra and an $R$-algebra compatibly, and which is the localisation of $R'$ at the multiplicative set generated by the images of the non-zero-divisors of $R$; let $k'$ be a field and $R$-algebra and $\psi \colon R' \to k'$ a homomorphism of $R$-algebras. Then there is a map $r$ from the set of those points $z \colon \operatorname{Spec} L \to B \times_{\operatorname{Spec} R} \operatorname{Spec} K$ over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $K \to L$ which are $n$-torsion for the base-changed group law `LB.genericFibre K` (that is, the $n$-fold multiple of $z$ is the unit) to the set of $n$-torsion points of $B$ over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $R \to k'$ for `LB`, such that $r$ is injective and, for every such $z$, there is an $n$-torsion point $x$ of $B$ over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $R \to R'$ with $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $R' \to L$ followed by $x$ equal to $z$ followed by the first projection $B \times_{\operatorname{Spec} R} \operatorname{Spec} K \to B$, and with $r(z)$ equal to $\operatorname{Spec} \psi$ followed by $x$.
--
--   This is the injectivity half of Serre–Tate's Lemma 2 on reduction of torsion of order invertible in the base, in a finite-level form: instead of the strict henselisation of $R$ one works with a single smooth $R$-algebra $R'$ whose localisation along $R$ is the field $L$ over which the torsion is rational, and the specialisation map is composition of the Néron extension with $\operatorname{Spec} \psi$. It is used by the corresponding statement for an unramified base extension, where the smoothness hypothesis on $R \to R'$ is replaced by unramifiedness at a point; injectivity rests on the rigidity statement [`GoodReductionJacobian.RelativeGroupLaw.eq_of_isTorsionPoint_of_comp_eq`](thm.html#GoodReductionJacobian.RelativeGroupLaw.eq_of_isTorsionPoint_of_comp_eq).
